A||||2||Homicidal||Replacing Dash Speakers.....||||5||
Z||000000||Homicidal||04-16-2000||12:13 PM||||I just ordered some new 4X10's. How do the dash speakers come out?  Can I just pop them in and out?  Or do I need to take apart the dash?<P>If it's really complicated I'll just have somebody do it.  But ever since I successfully hooked up my sub+amp, I'm feeling handy!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">||||reg||5
Z||000001||DXR_DAD||04-16-2000||12:33 PM||||Yep,,, one out one in, A thin screwdriver/tool and pop out the grills/covers, be careful not to force them to much, try to pull them up on all 4 corners with out breaking the tabs that hold them in!! they should come right out the top no need to remove anything on/under/in, dash||||reg||1
Z||000002||Ozzy||04-17-2000||01:48 AM||||Whut DXR_DAD said...Piece o' cake. <P>Little bit of fiddling round to determine phase of speakers. The old ones in my Coupe were not marked for polarity. You can look in your Haynes manual for the wire colors (+ & -)or use a small 1.5v battery to check which terminal is positive on the old speakers. Use a small piece of jumper wire and the battery by touching one end of the jumper to the neg. of the battery. Now place the jumper on a term. of your old speaker and touch the pos. end of the batt. to the other terminal. If the speaker moves forward, the pos. of the batt. is on the + term of the speaker. If the speaker cone moves inwards then the pos. term of the batt. is on the neg term of the speaker. Now make sure you remember which wires were on the old speaker terms and you can determine which wire is the positive.<P>P.S. dont hold the battery on the speaker terminals longer than you need to. You can damage the speaker.<P>Ozzy||||reg||1
